<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-exponential-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exponential Functions</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Evaluate and graph exponential functions.</p></li>
			<li><p>Solve exponential equations.</p></li>
			<li><p>Construct exponential models.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection>
    <title>Applications</title>

    <p>
      The skills we learn in this section will allow us to answer these questions:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given <m>A = 1000</m> and <m>r=0.04</m> and <m>t=5</m>, solve for <m>P</m> in <m>A=Pe^{rt}</m>. 
            (This is a finance problem, and the value of <m>P</m> answers the following question: 
            if an account earns <m>4</m> percent interest compounded continuously, 
            how much money should be deposited now so that the account balance is <m>\$1000</m> in <m>5</m> years?)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A biologist is growing a culture of bacteria. 
            The population of bacteria is modeled by the function <m>P(t) = 500e^{0.3t}</m>, where <m>t</m> is measured in hours. 
            How many bacteria are present after <m>10</m> hours?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            An archaeologist is studying the decay of a radioactive isotope.
            The amount of the isotope remaining after <m>t</m> years is modeled by the function <m>A(t) = 100e^{-0.00012t}</m>.
            How much of the isotope will remain after <m>10,000</m> years?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>Exponential functions</title>

    <p>
      <principle xml:id="exponential-function-untransformed">
        <title>Exponential function: untransformed</title>
        <p>
          An (untransformed) exponential function is a function of the form <m>f(x) = b^x</m>, where <m>b > 0</m> and <m>b \neq 1</m>.
          The number <m>b</m> is called the <term>base</term>.
        </p>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(x)=2^x</m> is an exponential function with base <m>2</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(x)=3^x</m> is an exponential function with base <m>3</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(x)=\pi^x</m> is an exponential function with base <m>\pi</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(x)=e^x</m> is an exponential function with base <m>e</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      We just gave four examples of exponential functions. 
      (We wanted to be specific, to point out that <m>b</m> as given in <xref ref="exponential-function-untransformed"/> should be a specific number, not a variable.)
      We also note that the base can be any positive real number other than <m>1</m>.
      Our first two exmples had integer bases.
      The third example <m>f(x)=\pi^x</m> has a non-integer base, with the familiar number <m>\pi \approx 3.14159</m>.
      The fourth example <m>f(x)=e^x</m> has a base of <m>e \approx 2.71828</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <principle xml:id="principle-advice-e">
        <title>Advice about <m>e</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            While the number <m>e</m> may not feel as familiar as <m>\pi</m>, to make it less intimidating, just remember that <m>e</m> is a constant, just like <m>\pi</m> is a constant (and just like <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> are constants, for that matter).
          </p>
          <p>
            In fact, it is helpful (and accurate!) to rememer that <m>\pi</m> is a number that is a little more than <m>3</m>, while <m>e</m> is a number that is a little less than <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      Finally, just to be clear about the distinction, <m>e</m> is a number (or a constant), but <m>f(x)=e^x</m> is a <em>function</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's examine <m>f(x)=2^x</m>, and build a table of values for <m>y=2^x</m> in order to graph this exponential function.
      <table>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{16}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{8}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>8</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>16</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      We explain several of the rows, just to be sure we understand how to compute the values of <m>y=2^x</m>.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When <m>x=4</m>, we have <m>y=2^4=2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 = 16</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When <m>x=-4</m>, we have <m>y=2^{-4}=\frac{1}{2^4}=\frac{1}{16}</m>.
            Going from <m>2^{-4}</m> to <m>\frac1{2^4}</m> applies <m>a^{-n} = \frac1{a^n}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-4"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When <m>x=0</m>, we have <m>y=2^0=1</m>. This applies <m>a^0=1</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-4"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      Based on this table, we can graph the function <m>f(x)=2^x</m>.
      <figure xml:id="fig-graph-of-2-to-the-x">
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:2.58,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{2^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=2^x</m></caption>
      </figure>      
      While having grid lines drawn in makes it easier to plot several of the points in the table and then graph,
      even without grid lines, the graph of <m>y=2^x</m> is a curve, and not a straight line.
      We can see this because we note that each one-unit increase in <m>x</m> doubles the value of <m>y</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly, a table for <m>y=3^x</m> is:
      <table>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{81}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{27}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{9}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>9</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>27</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>81</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>      
      which leads to the graph of <m>y=3^x</m>:
      <figure xml:id="fig-graph-of-3-to-the-x">
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:1.63,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{3^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=3^x</m></caption>
      </figure>     
    </p>

    <p>
      The graphs of <m>y=2^x</m> and <m>y=3^x</m> are similar. To see these graphs in context, we graph <m>y=2^x</m> and <m>y=3^x</m> together, but also include the graphs of <m>y=e^x</m> and <m>y=\pi^x</m>:
      <figure>
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, red, domain=-5.:2.58,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{2^\x});
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, orange, domain=-5.:1.79,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{e^\x});
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:1.63,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{3^\x});
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, brown, domain=-5.:1.56,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{pi^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=2^x</m> in red, <m>y=e^x</m> in orange, <m>y=3^x</m> in blue, and <m>y=\pi^x</m> in brown</caption>
      </figure>     
      The larger the base is, the steeper the graph is in the first quadrant, noting <m>2 &lt; e &lt; 3 &lt; \pi</m>.
      In the second quadrant, a larger base corresponds to a more shallow graph, one that is closer to the <m>x</m>-axis.
      Though all of these exponential function graphs appear to touch the <m>x</m>-axis, they never actually do.
      In <m>y=2^x</m>, for example, when <m>x=-10</m>, we have <m>y=2^{-10}=\frac{1}{2^{10}}=\frac{1}{1024} \approx 0.0009765625</m>.
      As <m>x</m> gets more and more negative, the value of <m>y</m> gets closer and closer to <m>0</m>, but it never actually reaches <m>0</m>.
      So, the range (the set of all possible <m>y</m>-values) of an exponential function in the format <m>y=b^x</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
      Because <m>a^0=1</m> for any base <m>a</m>, the graph of an exponential function in the format <m>y=b^x</m> will always pass through the point <m>(0,1)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      What happens when the base <m>b</m> satisfies <m>0 &lt; b &lt; 1</m>?
      Let's examine <m>f(x)=\left(\frac12\right)^x</m> and build a table of values for <m>y=\left(\frac12\right)^x</m> in order to graph this exponential function.
      <table>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>8</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac12</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac14</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac18</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      We explain several of the rows, just to be sure we understand how to compute the values of <m>y=\left(\frac12\right)^x</m>.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When <m>x=3</m>, we have <m>y=\left(\frac12\right)^3=\frac12 \cdot \frac12 \cdot \frac12 = \frac18</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When <m>x=-3</m>, we have <m>y=\left(\frac12\right)^{-3}=\frac{1}{\left(\frac12\right)^3}=\frac{1}{\frac18}=8</m>.
            Going from <m>\left(\frac12\right)^{-3}</m> to <m>\frac{1}{\left(\frac12\right)^3}</m> applies <m>a^{-n} = \frac1{a^n}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-4"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      Without presenting a separate table for <m>y=(\frac13)^x</m>, we graph these two exponential functions:
      <figure>
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, red, domain=-2.58:5,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{(1/2)^\x});
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.63:5,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{(1/3)^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=(\frac{1}{2})^x</m> in red, <m>y=(\frac{1}{3})^x</m> in blue</caption>
      </figure>          
      Due to the way negative exponents work, when the base is between <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>, the graph of the exponential function is steep in the second quadrant, and shallow in the first quadrant.
      These graphs also pass through the point <m>(0,1)</m> and have a range of <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We summarize properties about <m>y=b^x</m> (which hold whether <m>0 &lt; b &lt; 1</m> or whether <m>b &gt; 1</m>) as follows:
      <principle xml:id="principle-exponential-function-properties">
        <title>Properties of <m>y=b^x</m></title>
        <p>
          The graph of an exponential function in the format <m>y=b^x</m> has the following properties:
          <ul>
            <li><p>The graph passes through the point <m>(0,1)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The domain is <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The range is <m>(0,\infty)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The <m>x</m>-axis <m>y=0</m> is a horizontal asymptote.</p></li>
            <li><p>The function <m>f(x)=b^x</m> is one-to-one, since the graph <m>y=b^x</m> passes the horizontal line Test.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </principle>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Solving exponential equations</title>
    <p>
      Because <m>f(x)=b^x</m> is one-to-one, this gives us a technique for solving equations that have a certain shape to them:
      <principle xml:id="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>b^c=b^d</m>, then <m>c=d</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Given an equation where each side consists of a base and an exponent only, if the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example xml:id="example-solve-one-to-one-exponential">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>2^{7x+1} = 2^{3x-20}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Because the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:
            <m>7x+1 = 3x-20</m>. So <m>4x+1=-20</m>. So <m>4x=-21</m>. So <m>x=-\frac{21}{4}</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>3^{2x-1} = 3^{x+4}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Because the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:
            <m>2x-1 = x+4</m>. So <m>x-1=4</m>. So <m>x=5</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example xml:id="example-solve-one-to-one-exponential-change-base">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>2^{10x+2} = 8^{2x-1}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The bases are not the same, so we cannot apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> directly. However, we can replace <m>8</m> with <m>2^</m>.
            <me>2^{10x+2} = (2^3)^{2x-1}</me>
            We rewrite the right side using <m>(a^m)^n = a^{mn}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-1"/> to get
            <me>2^{10x+2} = 2^{3(2x-1)}</me>
            On the right side, we rewrite the exponent <m>3(2x-1)</m> by distributing <m>3</m> to get
            <me>2^{10x+2} = 2^{6x-3}</me>
            Now we can apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> to set the exponents equal to each other:
            <m>10x+2 = 6x-3</m>. So <m>4x+2=-3</m>. So <m>4x=-5</m>. So <m>x=-\frac{5}{4}</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      Note that we turned <m>8^{2x-1}</m> into <m>(2^3)^{2x-1}</m> because <m>8=2^3</m>. It would change the problem if we turned <m>2</m> into <m>2^3</m>, or if we turned <m>2</m> into <m>8</m>, or turned <m>8</m> into <m>2</m>.
      We made sure to replace <m>8</m> with something that it is equal to, namely <m>2^3</m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>5^{2x+1} = 125^{x-2}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The bases are not the same, so we cannot apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> directly. However, we can replace <m>125</m> with <m>5^3</m>.
            <me>5^{2x+1} = (5^3)^{x-2}</me>
            We rewrite the right side using <m>(a^m)^n = a^{mn}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-1"/> to get
            <me>5^{2x+1} = 5^{3(x-2)}</me>
            On the right side, we rewrite the exponent <m>3(x-2)</m> by distributing <m>3</m> to get
            <me>5^{2x+1} = 5^{3x-6}</me>
            Now we can apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> to set the exponents equal to each other:
            <m>2x+1 = 3x-6</m>. So <m>-x+1=-6</m>. So <m>-x=-7</m>. So <m>x=7</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the equation <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{6-x}=2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The bases are not the same, so we cannot apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> directly. However, we can replace <m>\frac12</m> with <m>2^{-1}</m>.
          <me>(2^{-1})^{6-x} = 2</me>
          We rewrite the left side using <m>(a^m)^n = a^{mn}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-1"/> to get
          <me>2^{-1(6-x)} = 2^1</me>
          On the left side, we rewrite the exponent <m>-1(6-x)</m> by distributing <m>-1</m> to get
          <me>2^{-6+x} = 2^1</me>
          Now we can apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> to set the exponents equal to each other:
          <m>-6+x = 1</m>. So <m>x=7</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>9^{2x}\cdot\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{x+2}=27\cdot(3^x)^{-2}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We rewrite everything to have a base of <m>3</m>:
            <me>(3^2)^{2x}\cdot(3^{-1})^{x+2} = 3^3\cdot(3^x)^{-2}</me>
            Apply <m>(a^m)^n = a^{mn}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-1"/> to get
            <me>3^{4x}\cdot3^{-x-2} = 3^3\cdot3^{-2x}.</me>
            Apply <m>a^m\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-1"/> to get
            <me>3^{4x-x-2} = 3^{3-2x}.</me>
            So
            <me>3^{3x-2} = 3^{3-2x}.</me>
            Now we can apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> to set the exponents equal to each other:
            <m>3x-2 = 3-2x</m>. So <m>5x-2=3</m>. So <m>5x=5</m>. So <m>x=1</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>x^2e^x + 10xe^x + 21e^x = 0</m>       
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We can factor out <m>e^x</m> from each term:
            <me>e^x(x^2 + 10x + 21) = 0</me>
            We factor further:
            <me>e^x(x + 3)(x + 7) = 0</me>
            Applying the extended Zero Product Property, 
            <me>e^x=0 \text{ or } x+3 = 0 \text{ or } x+7 = 0</me>
            Since <m>f(x)=e^x</m> has range <m>(0,\infty)</m>, we know that <m>e^x \neq 0</m> for any real number <m>x</m>, so <m>e^x=0</m> has no solution.
            So <m>x = -3</m> or <m>x = -7</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the <m>x</m>-intercepts of the function <m>f(x)=x^2(2e^{x})+4x(e^{x})+e^{x}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            To find <m>x</m>-intercepts, we set <m>y=0</m>, so:
            <me>0 = x^2(2e^{x})+4x(e^{x})+e^{x}</me>
            We can factor out <m>e^x</m> from each term:
            <me>0 = e^x(2x^2+4x+1)</me>
            Factoring further, we get
            <me>0 = e^x(2x+1)(x+1)</me>
            Applying the extended Zero Product Property,
            <me>e^x=0 \text{ or } 2x+1 = 0 \text{ or } x+1 = 0</me>
            Since <m>e^x</m> is never zero, the first equation <m>e^x=0</m> has no solution, so we must have
            <me>2x+1 = 0 \text{ or } x+1 = 0</me>
            So <m>x = -\frac{1}{2}</m> or <m>x = -1</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>General exponential functions and their graphs</title>

    <p>
      Recall that <m>f(x)=b^x</m> has fundamentally two shapes, based on whether <m>0 &lt; b &lt; 1</m> or <m>b &gt; 1</m>.
      <figure xml:id="fig-graph-of-b-to-the-x-b-greater-than-1">
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=b^x</m> when <m>b \gt 1</m></caption>
      </figure>     
      <figure xml:id="fig-graph-of-b-to-the-x-b-less-than-1">
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-3.37:5,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{(1/1.7)^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=b^x</m> when <m>0 \lt b \lt 1</m></caption>
      </figure>     
      We intentionally drew these without grid lines, to focus more on the general shape of the graphs, and less on the specific points.
      These graphs can be transformed (in the sense of graph transformations) to examine more general exponential functions:
      <principle xml:id="principle-exponential-function-general">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A (general) exponential function is a function of the form <m>f(x) = a \cdot b^x + c</m>, where <m>b \gt 0</m> and <m>b \neq 1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Graph the function <m>f(x) = 2^x + 3</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start with <m>y=2^x</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=2^x</m></caption>
            </figure>
            and shift this graph up <m>3</m> units to get the graph of <m>y = 2^x + 3</m>:                 
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x+2.5});
                      \draw[<->, dashed, gray, domain=-5.5:5.5] plot (\x,{2.5});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=2^x+3</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The dashed horizontal line is the horizontal asymptote <m>y=3</m>.
            Since the graph of <m>y=2^x</m> has a horizontal asymptote <m>y=0</m> whose dashed line is hidden by the solid <m>x</m>-axis, 
            the graph of <m>y=2^x + 3</m> has the horizontal asymptote <m>y=3</m>.
            Since <m>y=2^x</m> had <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,1)</m>, this point shifts up to <m>(0,4)</m> for the graph of <m>y=2^x + 3</m>.
          </p>          
        </answer>
      </example>

    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Graph the function <m>f(x) = 3^x - 5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start with <m>y=3^x</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=3^x</m></caption>
            </figure>
            and shift this graph down <m>5</m> units to get the graph of <m>y = 3^x - 5</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x-4});
                      \draw[<->, dashed, gray, domain=-5.5:5.5] plot (\x,{-4});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=3^x-5</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The dashed horizontal line is the horizontal asymptote <m>y=-4</m>.
            Since the graph of <m>y=3^x</m> has a horizontal asymptote <m>y=0</m> whose dashed line is hidden by the solid <m>x</m>-axis, 
            the graph of <m>y=3^x -4</m> has the horizontal asymptote <m>y=-4</m>.
            Since <m>y=3^x</m> had <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,1)</m>, this point shifts down to <m>(0,-4)</m> for the graph of <m>y=3^x - 5</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>      
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Graph the function <m>f(x) = 4e^x + 2</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start with <m>y=4^x</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=e^x</m></caption>
            </figure>
            and vertically stretch this by a factor of <m>4</m> to get <m>y=4e^x</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.8*1.7^\x});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=4e^x</m></caption>
            </figure>
            It is perhaps hard to tell, but for each point on the graph of <m>y=e^x</m>, the corresponding point on the graph of <m>y=4e^x</m> is four times as far from the <m>x</m>-axis.
            The horizontal asymptote remains <m>y=0</m>, and the <m>y</m>-intercept is now <m>(0,4)</m>.
            Finally, we shift this graph up <m>2</m> units to get the graph of <m>y = 4e^x + 2</m>:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.8*1.7^\x+1.5});
                      \draw[<->, dashed, gray, domain=-5.5:5.5] plot (\x,{1.5});
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=4e^x+2</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The dashed horizontal line is the horizontal asymptote <m>y=-2</m>.
            Since the graph of <m>y=4e^x</m> has a horizontal asymptote <m>y=0</m> whose dashed line is hidden by the solid <m>x</m>-axis, 
            the graph of <m>y=4e^x+2</m> has the horizontal asymptote <m>y=2</m>.
            Since <m>y=4e^x</m> had <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,4)</m>, this point shifts up to <m>(0,6)</m> for the graph of <m>y=4e^x + 2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>      
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            An exponential function has the form <m>f(x) = Ba^{-x} + C</m> in an application on exponential decay, where <m>f(x)</m> has a horizontal asymptote at <m>y=72</m>, has a <m>y</m>-intercept at <m>425</m>, and the graph passes through the point <m>(1,248.5)</m>.
            Find the values of <m>B</m>, <m>a</m>, and <m>C</m>. Then state the formula for <m>f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Since the horizontal asymptote is <m>y=72</m>, we know that <m>C=72</m>.
            Since the <m>y</m>-intercept is <m>425</m>, we know that <m>f(0)=425</m>. So
            <me>f(0) = B a^{-0} + 72 = B + 72 = 425</me>
            So <m>B=353</m>.
            Since the graph passes through the point <m>(1,248.5)</m>, we know that <m>f(1)=248.5</m>. So
            <me>f(1) = B a^{-1} + 72 = \frac{B}{a} + 72 = \frac{353}{a} + 72 = 248.5</me>
            So <m>\frac{353}{a} = 176.5</m>. So <m>a=2</m>.
            Therefore, the formula for this exponential function is
            <me>f(x) = 353 \cdot 2^{-x} + 72.</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Applications Revisited</title>

    <p>
      Let's revisit the applications we introduced at the beginning of this section.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>A = 1000</m> and <m>r=0.04</m> and <m>t=5</m>, solve for <m>P</m> in <m>A=Pe^{rt}</m>. 
            (This is a finance problem, and the value of <m>P</m> answers the following question: 
            if an account earns <m>4</m> percent interest compounded continuously, 
            how much money should be deposited now so that the account balance is <m>\$1000</m> in <m>5</m> years?)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <me>P = \frac{A}{e^{rt}} = \frac{1000}{e^{0.04 \cdot 5}} \approx 1000 / 1.2214 \approx 818.73</me>
            So <m>818.73</m> dollars should be deposited now.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A biologist is growing a culture of bacteria. 
            The population of bacteria is modeled by the function <m>P(t) = 500e^{0.3t}</m>, where <m>t</m> is measured in hours. 
            How many bacteria are present after <m>10</m> hours?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <me>P(10) = 500e^{0.3 \cdot 10} = 500e^3 \approx 500 \cdot 20.0855 \approx 10042.75</me>
            So there are approximately <m>10043</m> bacteria present after <m>10</m> hours.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            An archaeologist is studying the decay of a radioactive isotope.
            The amount of the isotope remaining after <m>t</m> years is modeled by the function <m>A(t) = 100e^{-0.00012t}</m>.
            How much of the isotope will remain after <m>10,000</m> years?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <me>A(10000) = 100e^{-0.00012 \cdot 10000} = 100e^{-1.2} \approx 100 \cdot 0.3012 \approx 30.12</me>
            So there are approximately <m>30</m> units of the isotope remaining after <m>10,000</m> years.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>

</section>